==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|processioun|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|pourciession|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|processio||a marching forward, an advance, in Late Latin a religious procession|lang=la}}, from {{term|procedere}}, past participle {{term|processus||to move forward, advance, proceed}}; see [[proceed]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-procession.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The act of [[progressing]] or [[proceeding]].
#: {{rfquotek|Bishop Pearson}}
#* Trench
#*: That the '''procession''' of their life might be / More equable, majestic, pure, and free.
# A [[group]] of people or things moving along in an [[orderly]], stately, or solemn manner; a train of persons advancing in order; a [[retinue]].
#: ''a '''procession''' of mourners; the Lord Mayor's '''procession'''''
#* Shakespeare
#*: the townsmen on '''procession'''
# A number of things [[happening]] in [[sequence]] (in [[space]] or in [[time]]).
# {{context|ecclesiastical|obsolete|in the plural|lang=en}} [[litany|Litanies]] which were said in procession and not kneeling.
#: {{rfquotek|Shipley}}

